  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 60af0432a3c869e48a591344a8dafd71, type: 3}
  m_Name: S&EDisc 4
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  question: "Your research team is narrowing down which cancer drug candidates should
    move forward to preclinical animal studies next. A new graduate student, who
    is observing your team\u2019s early lab work, is curious whether the animal care
    committee has to review the project now, even though no animal studies have started
    yet.\n\nWhen does the Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) need
    to be involved?"
  explanation: "The Institutional Animal Care and Use Committee (IACUC) oversees
    research involving animals, but they don\u2019t review early lab experiments
    that happen before animals are involved. Their review begins when a team plans
    to use live animals, making sure the studies are humane, safe, and scientifically
    justified. They also check that animals are cared for properly throughout the
    study, including housing, feeding, and minimizing pain or stress."
  correctAnswer: 'B: They only get involved when the team begins planning or conducting
    actual animal studies.'
  incorrectAnswer1: 'A: They must approve every lab experiment even before animals
    are used.'
  incorrectAnswer2: 'C: They only review the results of the research after animal
    studies are complete.'
  incorrectAnswer3: 'D: They must review and approve human studies after the animal
    studies are complete.'
  questionRole: Safety & Ethics
  questionType: MultipleChoice
  questionStage: Discovery
